.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause
.Dd October 11, 2026
.Dt CAPSIGN 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm capsign
.Nd embed, sign, and inspect program policy
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm key-id
.Fl p Ar public.pem
.Nm
.Cm note
.Fl m Ar policy.ucl
.Fl o Ar note.S
.Nm
.Cm sign
.Op Fl n
.Fl k Ar private.pem
.Op Fl m Ar policy.ucl
.Op Fl o Ar signed-image
.Ar image
.Nm
.Cm verify
.Op Fl c
.Op Fl p Ar public.pem
.Ar image ...
.Nm
.Cm show
.Op Fl p Ar public.pem
.Ar image
.Nm
.Cm inspect
.Op Fl p Ar public.pem
.Ar image
.Nm
.Cm validate-policy
.Ar policy.ucl
.Nm
.Cm resign
.Op Fl n
.Fl k Ar new-private.pem
.Op Fl p Ar old-public.pem
.Op Fl o Ar new-image
.Ar image
.Nm
.Cm resign
.Fl r
.Op Fl n
.Fl k Ar new-private.pem
.Op Fl p Ar old-public.pem
.Op Fl o Ar new-tree
.Ar bundle-or-tree
.Nm
.Cm remove
.Op Fl r
.Fl o Ar new-output
.Ar image-or-tree
.Nm
.Cm seal
.Fl k Ar private.pem
.Fl d Ar domain
.Fl o Ar signature
.Ar artifact
.Nm
.Cm check
.Op Fl p Ar public.pem
.Fl d Ar domain
.Fl s Ar signature
.Ar artifact
.Nm
.Cm bundle-sign
.Fl k Ar private.pem
.Ar bundle.cap
.Nm
.Cm bundle-verify
.Op Fl p Ar public.pem
.Ar bundle.cap
.Nm
.Cm sign
.Fl r
.Fl k Ar private.pem
.Op Fl o Ar new-tree
.Ar bundle-or-tree
.Nm
.Cm verify
.Fl r
.Op Fl p Ar public.pem
.Ar bundle-or-tree
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The inspect command verifies an image before emitting a JSON object with
schema org.5bsd.capsign.image.v1, the canonical container type, signature
algorithm, domain-separated digest algorithm, selected verification-key
source, policy byte count, public-key identifier and signed digest.
It emits no inspection object for unsigned, malformed or untrusted input.
An explicit verification key establishes the selected signer's identity;
it does not establish permission to grant runtime attributes.
Use show to extract the authenticated policy bytes.
Verification normally stops at the first failing image.
With
.Fl c
or
.Fl -continue ,
it checks every image operand, reports each failure on standard error,
and returns failure if any image failed.
This option applies to non-recursive image verification.
Options that do not apply to the selected command are usage errors.
.Pp
The key-id command prints the raw Ed25519 public-key identifier used in the
system trust catalogue.
.Pp
The note command emits target assembler containing the exact policy bytes
in a non-allocated .5bsd.policy build-input section.
Link this assembler with the program, complete stripping and other binary
transformations, then sign the final image.
The source image is not modified and published output files must not
already exist.
Outside dry-run mode,
.Fl o
is required.
For non-recursive image signing,
.Fl n
or
.Fl -dry-run
copies, embeds and signs a private temporary image, then removes it without
publishing an output.
.Fl o
is optional in this mode; when supplied it selects the directory for temporary
work, and any existing destination remains untouched.
Without it, temporary work uses /tmp.
Dry-run validates the signing operation, not whether a later publication can
succeed; it does not reserve an output name or validate output nonexistence.
.Pp
For an ordinary linked ELF binary without a build-input section, pass
.Fl m
(or
.Fl -manifest )
to sign.
The tool appends the exact policy, signature header and a bounded footer without
moving existing program headers or load segments.
The signature is a non-allocated
.Pa .note.5bsd.signature
ELF note; copied section metadata and an authenticated original-header
record permit exact signature removal.
The redundant footer must match the note.
Use
.Xr readelf 1
to inspect the section and note.
The whole file, including the footer, is authenticated.
Both build-time and standalone policy inputs produce this single final
signature representation, outside every ELF segment.
When a policy is already embedded, an explicitly supplied manifest must match
its bytes exactly.
Complete stripping and debug-link operations before either signing workflow.
.Pp
The verify and show commands authenticate the image before reporting identity
or printing policy bytes.
Without an explicit public key they use the system key catalogue described in
.Xr libcapimage 3 .
An unsigned build-input section is not trusted.
There is no permissive verification mode.
.Pp
The resign command rotates the key of a signed standalone image.
It authenticates a private copy using the old public key or system trust
catalogue, preserves the exact embedded policy and original permission bits,
and signs that copy with the new private key.
Unsigned, damaged or untrusted input is rejected before publication.
Manifest replacement is not accepted by this command.
It requires a new output except with
.Fl n
or
.Fl -dry-run ,
which exercises verification and signing without publishing an output.
.Pp
The resign -r command verifies a private copy of an already signed tree using
the old public key or system trust catalogue, preserves each executable's
policy, and signs and seals nested bundles inside out with the new key.
It requires a new output and does not change the source tree.
.Pp
The remove command writes a new unsigned copy, removing the signature container
and its authenticated policy while restoring the original ELF bytes.
It requires no signing key and refuses an existing output.
With -r it also removes nested and enclosing bundle seals in the copied tree.
The source remains unchanged; removal does not grant authority to the output.
.Pp
The seal and check commands authenticate separate immutable artifacts.
Their protocol domain prevents a signature for one artifact kind from being
reused for another kind.
.Pp
The bundle-sign command seals a sorted inventory of the bundle metadata,
unit manifests, executable images, resources, directory structure, and modes.
Sign executable images first; this command creates
.Pa Signature/Seal
and refuses to overwrite an existing seal.
The bundle-verify command requires a valid inventory seal and valid embedded
image signatures.
Runtime policy comes exclusively from each signed executable;
external Unit.ucl files are not read as policy.
.Pp
The
.Fl r
(or
.Fl -recursive )
option to sign or bundle-sign signs every declared native unit program in each
capability bundle under the input tree, then seals and verifies bundles from the
inside out.
It requires a new output directory and leaves the source tree unchanged.
Each unit retains its own embedded policy; a unit without embedded policy uses
its Unit.ucl file.
A global manifest override is rejected for recursive operations.
Unsigned build inputs are checked for agreement before recursive signing.
Input trees must be quiescent and unsealed; symlinks, special files and set-ID
files are rejected.
Directories in the private copy are made owner-accessible; regular file modes
are preserved.
The output root remains mode 0700.
For a single bundle, the output name must also end in .cap.
On failure, remove the incomplete output tree before retrying.
Recursive verify checks every nested bundle seal and its declared images;
it does not modify the tree.
.Pp
Each signed unit bin directory must contain exactly one regular executable
with an embedded policy; Unit.ucl is unnecessary at runtime.
A shared executable can carry a profiles object keyed by
.Ar bundle_id/unit_name ;
each selected profile is a complete unit policy.
.Pp
Base program Makefiles can set
.Va CAPIMAGE_MANIFEST
to a policy or profile document.
The standard program rules emit and link the build-input section without
executing a target program or accessing a signing key.
Complete stripping and installation before signing a staged generation.
Standalone stripping must use --keep-section=.5bsd.policy to retain the build
input, or the policy must be supplied again with sign -m.
The maintained base installation rules preserve the input automatically.
Unsigned development sections do not grant authority; development bundle loading
still requires the external Unit.ucl policy.
Creating the root-owned, non-writable-to-others regular file
.Pa /etc/capability/require-signatures
requires sealed bundles and trusted image signatures during bundle loading.
.Ss Offline policy validation
The validate-policy command checks a standalone unit manifest using the same
strict schema validator as SwitchBoard.
It rejects duplicate and unknown keys, invalid field types and program names,
external includes, NUL bytes and documents larger than one MiB.
The input must be a regular file, not a symlink.
Validation requires no signing key and does not modify the input.
It does not authenticate code, grant attributes, resolve resource references,
or prove that a complete bundle is deployable.
Use recursive signing or verification to validate a complete bundle generation.
.Ss Recursive dry runs
The sign -r and resign -r commands accept
.Fl n
or
.Fl -dry-run .
They perform complete signing and verification on a private temporary copy,
including nested bundles, then remove the copy without publishing a tree.
Resign verifies the old signatures before replacing them in the private copy.
The source remains unchanged.
With
.Fl o ,
the proposed output must be a new path; scratch space is created in its parent.
Without an output path, scratch space uses TMPDIR or /tmp.
Ordinary failures also clean up the private copy; forced process termination
can leave a private scratch directory requiring removal.
A successful dry run does not activate code or install trust keys.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
openssl genpkey -algorithm ed25519 -out private.pem
openssl pkey -in private.pem -pubout -out public.pem
capsign note -m manifest.ucl -o policy.S
cc program.c policy.S -o program
strip program
capsign sign -k private.pem -o program.signed program
capsign verify -p public.pem program.signed
capsign show -p public.pem program.signed
# A prebuilt ELF binary:
capsign sign -m manifest.ucl -k private.pem -o tool.signed tool
# A bundle, retaining each unit's own policy:
capsign sign -r -k private.pem -o Signed.cap Input.cap
capsign verify -r -p public.pem Signed.cap
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr libcapimage 3 ,
.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
Private signing keys belong in the release signing environment.
Do not ship them in a package or guest image.
Signature validity is separate from permission to assert an attribute.
The note command preserves policy bytes; it does not validate their schema.
Runtime policy consumers must use the strict manifest parser.
